# Line endings are LF everywhere, including on Windows checkouts (FRE-160).
#
# Without this, git's default `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows rewrites every
# text file to CRLF on checkout, which breaks three things at once:
#
#   1. Tests that compare a checked-in file byte for byte — the round-trip
#      fixture in tests/config_back_compat.rs, and the `include_str!` mirrors
#      in tests/file_associations.rs that match on "</key>\n\t<true/>".
#      They fail on a difference git introduced, not one the code made.
#   2. Any future test over the seven files read with `include_str!`
#      (Cargo.toml, Dioxus.toml, release.yml, the three under packaging/,
#      README.md), which is a standing invitation to the same bug.
#
# Not a reason, though it looks like one: the macOS bundle. `info_plist_path`
# does copy packaging/macos/Info.plist verbatim, but release.yml builds that
# bundle on macos-latest, where git never converts — and XML tolerates either
# ending regardless. The byte-exact tests are the whole justification.
#
# The repo has no file that wants CRLF — no .bat or .cmd, and nothing checked
# in with CRLF today — so a single rule is enough and there is no exception
# list to keep current.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Fixtures are data, not source. Today there is exactly one — an ASCII TOML
# compared for exact bytes — but a fixture directory attracts binary files
# (a SQLite database is the obvious next one), and `-text` is the rule that
# stays correct for both: "never convert" is stronger than "normalise to LF",
# and it is what a byte-exact comparison wants either way.
#
# It also makes this the one place a CRLF file could legitimately live: a
# fixture authored on Windows keeps its CRLF bytes on every platform, which
# is the point of pinning bytes rather than endings.
tests/fixtures/** -text
